Third-party projects
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This repository contains NO third-party source code. It vendors nothing.

The following open-source projects are STUDIED AND CITED because the analyzed
bypass is built on top of them. They are referenced by name in the
documentation and in detection-rule metadata so that defenders can tell stock
upstream behaviour from behaviour added by the bypass. No code from any of them
is copied into this repository, in source or binary form, so their terms -
including any copyleft terms - place no obligation on this work. Their
licences are deliberately NOT restated here: the authoritative licence is the
one published in each upstream repository, and that is where anyone intending
to reuse their code should look.

  - SimpleSvm - Satoshi Tanda
    https://github.com/tandasat/SimpleSvm
    A minimal educational AMD SVM hypervisor. The bypass ships a driver derived
    from it. Referenced in defense/telemetry/yara/denuvowo.yar, whose strings
    are lifecycle log messages published in that project's public source, not
    extracted from the bypass binary.

  - HyperDbg - Sina Karvandi
    https://github.com/HyperDbg/HyperDbg
    A hypervisor-assisted debugger. The bypass's VMM component is reported to
    be based on it. Referenced by name only.

  - EfiGuard - Mattiwatti
    https://github.com/Mattiwatti/EfiGuard
    A UEFI bootkit that disables PatchGuard and driver signature enforcement.
    Present in sibling releases of the same family, absent from the flow
    analyzed here. Referenced by name only.

